WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.002
Clickjacking happens when an attacker frames a legitimate page and tricks a user into clicking or interacting with it in a misleading context.

2
00:00:09.002 --> 00:00:15.903
The user believes they are clicking one thing, but the framed application receives a different interaction.

3
00:00:15.903 --> 00:00:24.679
This risk is especially important for pages that change settings, submit transactions, approve actions, or expose sensitive workflows.

4
00:00:24.679 --> 00:00:29.705
Browser framing controls help the application decide where it may be embedded.

5
00:00:29.705 --> 00:00:34.656
X-Frame-Options is an older but still common header for controlling framing.

6
00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:38.107
DENY tells browsers that the page should not be framed.

7
00:00:38.107 --> 00:00:41.707
SAMEORIGIN allows framing by pages from the same origin.

8
00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:46.883
These settings are simple and useful for many applications, but they are limited.

9
00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:55.585
They do not express complex partner allowlists well, and modern policy design often uses CSP frame-ancestors for more flexible control.

10
00:00:55.585 --> 00:01:02.036
The frame-ancestors directive in Content Security Policy controls which origins may frame the page.

11
00:01:02.036 --> 00:01:12.612
It can support more precise policies for applications that have legitimate embedding needs, such as partner portals, internal dashboards, or integrated workflows.

12
00:01:12.612 --> 00:01:17.113
The key is to decide intentionally which pages can be framed and by whom.

13
00:01:17.113 --> 00:01:25.290
Public login pages, account settings, administrative actions, and payment flows usually deserve strict framing protections.

14
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:31.291
Avoid accidental breakage by understanding legitimate embeds before enforcing a new policy.

15
00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:38.567
Some applications depend on internal iframes, partner integrations, identity flows, or documentation portals.

16
00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:43.293
A good rollout tests representative workflows and documents exceptions.

17
00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:48.994
The safest framing policy is the narrowest one that still supports required business use.

18
00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:52.219
Anything broader should have an owner and a reason.
